32:13.448 --> 32:24.359
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for me, when I fly a lighter fabric, it might fly better or it might talk to you better in the air. But with the amount of money that goes

32:24.399 --> 32:33.483
[SPEAKER_00]: into investing... into buying a wing, I want it to last a bit longer. So that's why I end up going towards heavier and more durable fabrics. But

32:33.704 --> 32:40.807
[SPEAKER_00]: if money is not a problem and you're just looking for performance, and I'm not saying X helps people, like they fly gliders, which are probably

32:40.887 --> 32:51.912
[SPEAKER_00]: built to last two weeks or a few months at max. What is your take on lightweight fabrics, lightweight designs versus regular weight

32:51.932 --> 33:01.216
[SPEAKER_00]: designs and which one feels better? In the last five years, Light fabric has been enhanced a lot. And there is new technology in getting those

33:01.296 --> 33:17.284
[SPEAKER_01]: light clothes. I remember having Mantra 6 at the time. After 300 or 400 hours, the porosity test had over 270 seconds. And that was a light

33:17.344 --> 33:30.342
[SPEAKER_01]: version. The Mantra 6 light, not the normal. The light materials have evolved a lot. And manufacturers are using them because also I think

33:30.402 --> 33:41.368
[SPEAKER_01]: they are matching more the collapsing behavior. I think they are smoother. They are easier to fly. You don't have the inertia and they are

33:41.408 --> 33:51.373
[SPEAKER_01]: not heavy. When you have a high pitch, they are smoother than regular clothes. And of course, they launch better and pack better. I like them a

33:51.413 --> 34:03.728
[SPEAKER_01]: lot. So I think, yes, I think light fabric, if someone want to keep a glider with the light fabric, it depends on how light it is today. You

34:03.768 --> 34:12.136
[SPEAKER_01]: don't have hard Mormon fabric and you don't have a light fabric. You have maybe two kind of three light, three kind of light fabric. You have maybe

34:12.696 --> 34:22.946
[SPEAKER_01]: 20 grand and you have 40 grand and you have, I don't know. Yeah. In the middle is okay, but to be on a glider that weights around four kilos today

34:22.986 --> 34:34.275
[SPEAKER_01]: is fair enough. What's your take on lines? Because lines are sheeted, unsheated, quite a lot of technology in lines, and there's a lot of

34:34.395 --> 34:43.783
[SPEAKER_00]: endless combinations of lines. So what are your thoughts about lines? Yeah, paragliding lines, you have Kevlar lines and then Dyneema lines.

34:45.173 --> 34:58.240
[SPEAKER_01]: Both of them can be used with a mixture in one glider because one gets shrinked and the other one gets damaged. But the lines don't change.

34:58.260 --> 35:09.266
[SPEAKER_01]: They are the same. All the gliders are using the same lines, the same line, the same specifications. But the pilots must know that even a high

35:09.287 --> 35:23.552
[SPEAKER_01]: B glider or a B glider or a C glider, of course, D glider, will change after 20 or 30 hours. So if I get a new glider today, I test fly it, after 20 hours

35:23.873 --> 35:36.781
[SPEAKER_01]: or 30 hours, the trim will change. And yeah, sometimes it will change and maybe change to a better glider or maybe slower glider because all the

35:36.821 --> 35:46.065
[SPEAKER_01]: manufacturers are using the same line lengths also. They're very similar. If I get Ten gliders now in the B class, you put the lines next to

35:46.106 --> 35:58.020
[SPEAKER_01]: each other, there's no difference other than the colors. I see the Reflex profile coming into paragliders as well. I think BGD launched

35:58.180 --> 36:08.525
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflex in Diva. Does that help with trimming or less of trimming requirement? Or how does Reflex impact paragliders? No, but there is no

36:08.565 --> 36:17.809
[SPEAKER_01]: relation, I think, I don't know, no relation between Reflex and trimming. Reflex makes a profile less collapsible. Of course, you need

36:17.989 --> 36:30.605
[SPEAKER_01]: specific lines to collapse it. And the trimming is related to the line. It's not a reflex. I was thinking maybe paramotor gliders don't require

36:30.645 --> 36:40.930
[SPEAKER_00]: trimming every 30, 40 hours. So that's why my curiosity stemmed from that fact. Yeah, I think paramotor wings have all sheeted lines, big

36:41.070 --> 36:51.941
[SPEAKER_01]: sheeted lines. And you have trimmers also. So this helps if you get maybe after 50 or 60 or 100 hours, you can release the trimmers and maybe 50%

36:52.842 --> 37:03.692
[SPEAKER_01]: will solve the problem. But in gliders like the Diva, they use thin lines like the Zeno 2. They use thin lines and really small lines. And those

37:03.752 --> 37:05.674
[SPEAKER_01]: lines require trimming after 20 or 30 hours.

37:09.490 --> 37:20.877
[SPEAKER_00]: Can it be dangerous if you don't trim your glider for 100 or 200 hours? If you never trim it, can it become a risky glider to fly? I think it depends

37:21.017 --> 37:32.645
[SPEAKER_01]: on the class. I mean, I have seen low B gliders never being trimmed for 500 hours and still fly as well. Maybe a little bit slower, but it depends

37:32.725 --> 37:41.593
[SPEAKER_01]: also on the manufacturer. It depends on the lines. but they they will be slower sometimes they will maybe would be difficult to launch

37:41.954 --> 37:52.643
[SPEAKER_01]: difficult to fly but that's why you should train them all the time or maybe two times or three times per lifetime Fair enough. I see you have

37:52.683 --> 38:04.826
[SPEAKER_00]: started reviewing harnesses as well off lately in your blog spot. Harnesses come in a variety of shapes and sizes, and it also sticks to

38:04.886 --> 38:14.929
[SPEAKER_00]: personal preference. But usually when you come across harnesses, what are some things that define a harness which make them better than the

38:14.969 --> 38:24.125
